<header class="hero"><p class="eyebrow">HUMAN GOLD RUSH LESSON</p><h1>Community Force</h1><p class="intro">A pledge names a future intention. Trust makes coordinated action possible; fulfillment places that force into motion.</p><blockquote>How can a community recognize trade value without turning gratitude into money?</blockquote></header><aside class="notice"><strong>Important distinction</strong><p>Any reference to 103 describes pledged community trade value used for learning—not cash held, currency issued, or a guaranteed financial benefit.</p></aside><section class="content-grid"><article><span>01</span><div><h2>Seeking Gratitude</h2><p>A product, service, contribution, or act may be recognized with gratitude. That recognition is not accepted as money, payment, credit, stored value, or a financial claim.</p></div></article><article><span>02</span><div><h2>YAM-is-ON</h2><p>A separate trading voucher may signal that pledged trade value is maturing through a twelve-week window. The voucher is not money.</p></div></article><article><span>03</span><div><h2>YAM JAM</h2><p>Actual money movement belongs to the separate YAM JAM monetary and settlement environment after fulfillment. It is not part of the LLB gratitude experience.</p></div></article><article><span>04</span><div><h2>The clear distinction</h2><p>Human Gold Rush discovers value. YAM-is-ON matures trade. YAM JAM moves money.</p></div></article><article><span>05</span><div><h2>Pledge tiers, not price tags</h2><p>A MEGAvoter membership request is recorded as a $42 ($12/$30) pledge — a way of testing what human-capital intent does to build trust in a community, with absolutely no intention of ever collecting the money. The pilot involves no money, full stop. A separate Organized Krill kit pledge (hat, shirt, and a 10-pack of hang tags/stickers) works the same way: a research pledge value, not a price.</p></div></article><article><span>06</span><div><h2>Ten-packs in circulation</h2><p>Hang tags and stickers move through the community in 10-packs so distribution itself becomes a countable, observable event — who carried them, who handed them off, and who scanned one in.</p></div></article></section><section class="media-section"><div class="section-heading"><p>MEDIA FOR THIS LESSON</p><h2>Watch, listen, and discuss</h2></div><div class="media-grid"><article class="media-card video"><div class="media-frame"><span>▶</span><small>VIDEO 1</small></div><h3>Community Force: orientation</h3><p>Reserved for the featured story or introductory video.</p><a href="#">Transcript coming soon</a></article><article class="media-card video"><div class="media-frame"><span>▶</span><small>VIDEO 2</small></div><h3>Community conversation</h3><p>Reserved for a demonstration, conversation, or local example.</p><a href="#">Discussion guide coming soon</a></article><article class="media-card podcast"><div class="media-frame"><span>◉</span><small>PODCAST</small></div><h3>Listen and reflect</h3><p>Reserved for the facilitator or community podcast episode.</p><a href="#">Transcript coming soon</a></article></div></section>